Model raw response: <|channel|>analysis<|message|>**High‑level goal**

The user wants a tiny schematic that just shows a single Schottky rectifier (the 1N5819 part from the “Diode” library) used to rectify an AC input. The schematic must use the *exact* part name and library, so the code will only contain that component, a single AC source, and a ground reference. No extra components, no substitutions.

**Physical layout**

* The schematic sheet is 210 mm × 297 mm (A4).  
* We place the circuit near the centre of the sheet to keep the drawing tidy.  
* The centre of the 1N5819 symbol will be at **(150 mm, 110 mm)** – that’s comfortably in the middle of the page.  
* The AC source (a 5 V peak‑to‑peak sine generator) is drawn a little to the left of the diode, while the ground symbol is drawn a little to the right.  
* All components are kept at least a few millimetres apart so their bounding boxes never overlap.

**Component selection and symbol parameters**

| Symbol | Library | Reference | Value | Rotation | Mirror |
|--------|---------|-----------|-------|----------|--------|
| 1N5819 | “Diode” | D1 | “1N5819” | **90°** | None |
| AC source | “Jumper / AC_Sine” | VIN | “VIN” | 0° | None |
| Ground | “power / GND” | GND | “GND” | 0° | None |

The 1N5819 symbol has two pins: **Anode (A)** on the right side and **Cathode (K)** on the left side. Rotating the symbol by 90° turns the Anode to point downwards, which makes it easier to wire the rectifier’s anode to the AC source and the cathode to ground.

**Pin locations**

* `D1.A` – Anode pin (pin name “A”); after a 90° rotation it lies on the bottom side of the symbol.  
* `D1.K` – Cathode pin (pin name “K”); after the rotation it lies on the top side.  
* `VIN.S` – Source pin of the AC sine generator (pin name “S”).  
* `GND.S` – Ground pin (pin name “S”).

Using `get_pin_location` we obtain the exact (x, y) coordinates of each pin relative to the symbol centre. These coordinates are the starting points for the wires.

**Wire routing**

1. **VIN → D1.A** – Connect the source pin of the AC generator to the diode’s anode.  
   * `connect_pins("VIN", "S", "D1", "A")`  
   * The wire starts at the AC source pin and ends at the diode’s anode.

2. **D1.K → GND** – Connect the diode’s cathode to the ground reference.  
   * `connect_pins("D1", "K", "GND", "S")`  
   * The wire starts at the diode’s cathode and ends at the ground pin.

Because the diode is the only active element, no other wires are needed. The AC source is fed directly into the rectifier, and the rectified DC appears at the diode’s cathode, which is tied to ground.

**Why the chosen rotation?**

Rotating the diode 90° makes the Anode (normally on the right) point downwards. This orientation lets the AC source sit to the left and the ground to the right, creating a straight‑line left‑to‑right flow of power that is visually intuitive. It also keeps the label text from overlapping the symbol’s bounding box.

**Why no mirroring?**

Mirroring would flip the symbol horizontally or vertically, which would misalign the pin names with the expected layout and would require additional wire routing. Leaving the symbol unmirrored preserves the standard pin positions.

**Final wire write‑out**

After all `connect_pins` calls, `write_out_all_wires()` is executed. This function collects the wire data and writes the schematic file, ensuring that the connections appear correctly in KiCad.

---

